# Task 1 - one number per process.
#
# A Lamport timestamp is a single counter. A send increments the sender's
# counter and carries it along. A receive takes the larger of the two and
# then increments. That is the whole rule.
#
# It buys one guarantee, worth stating precisely:
#
#     if a happened before b, then L(a) < L(b)
#
# The converse is not true, and finding out why is the point of this task.

@process
class Node:
    pass

p1 = Node(speed=1.0)      # step 4: make it 0.3
p2 = Node(speed=1.0)
p3 = Node(speed=1.0)

# --- the world -------------------------------------------------------

world = World(machines=[p1, p2, p3])

# --- the job ---------------------------------------------------------

def story() -> void:
    p1.send(p2, "hello")
    p2.send(p3, "onwards")
    p3.send(p1, "reply")
    # step 2: uncomment. p2 has not heard from p3 at this point, and p3 has
    # not heard about this — yet both get a number, and one is smaller
    # p2.send(p1, "and again")

# `clock="lamport"` is what makes the stamps single numbers. Take it away and
# the same chat runs under vector clocks, which is Task 2.
job = Calls(run=story, clock="lamport")

world.run(job)
